samples.guided_completion:
The callback function is called when the assertion fails. This allows you to inspect the error and possibly take further action.

For example, if you want to log the error message, you can modify the callback function to do so:

void onAssertionError(const AssertionError& error) {
  print("Assertion error: $error");
}

In this way, you can handle the error and possibly log it to a file or console.

Another use case is when you want to ensure that the assertion is only triggered under certain conditions. For example, you might only want to trigger the assertion when the dynamic parameter is not null.

Here's an example:

void testDynamicParam() {
  expect(cannotBeNull(null), throwsAssertionError);
  expect(cannotBeNull('test'), throwsAssertionError);
  expect(cannotBeNull({a: 1})), throwsAssertionError);
}

In this example, the assertion is only triggered when the dynamic parameter is null, 'test' is not null, and {a: 1} is not null.

