samples.general_completion:
Finish the second part of the passage based on the first part, ensuring that these two pieces become a coherent passage.

The reason for this behavior is that Java generics are type-safe, meaning that the type of a generic type parameter must be known at compile time. When you define an interface with a generic type parameter, the compiler checks that the type parameter is actually a type that can be instantiated according to the generic type declaration. In your case, `SomeInterface1<T extends Type0>` is treated as `Type0` because `T` is a subtype of `Type0`. The `extends Type0` part of the interface definition ensures that `T` must be a subtype of `Type0`.

To resolve this issue, you can explicitly specify the type parameter in the interface definition. For example:

```java
public interface SomeInterface0<T extends Type0> {
    T get();
}

public interface SomeInterface1<T extends Type1> extends SomeInterface0<T> { }
```

Now, `SomeInterface1<T extends Type1>` will be treated as `Type1`, and you can use it as expected:

```java
... SomeInterface1 si1;
...
si1.get().method();
```

This ensures that the type parameter is explicitly specified

